window.KB_BODY[32]={"tsb210269":"<h2>Engine Will Not Crank or Cranks Slowly (Electric Starter): Debris and Dust in Electric Starting Motor</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM850</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Engine will <b>not</b> crank or cranks slowly (electric starter)</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Nose bushing of electric starting motor contamination causing nose bushing and drive end bushing wear</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Visual inspection of electric starting motor can confirm if dust and debris has collected in nose cone of electric starting motor where slow cranking or no cranking of the engine is experienced.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If dust and debris has collected in the nose cone of the electric starting motor, a noseless electric starting motor option is available. See Table 1 for part number information.</li><li>For proper removal and install instructions, see corresponding QST30 Service Manual. Reference Procedure 013-020 in Section 13.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Motor, Starting</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3651893\">3651893</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>4371902</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37287882</td><td>July 2022</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220018":"<h2>New Eliminator™ Lubricating Oil Filter Assembly</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KV38 All</li><li>KV50 All</li><li>QSK38 All</li><li>QSK45 All</li><li>QSK50 All</li><li>QSK60 All</li><li>QSK78 All</li><li>QSK95 All</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>New Eliminator™ Assemblies are being released with revised internals such as an improved hydraulic motor, centrifuge, and new filtering element screens which have a finer mesh screen to improve filtering capability.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>The filter element screens are no longer purchased in pairs, all elements must now be replaced as a full assembly.</li><li>The cleaning and inspecting procedure for the filter element screens is now <b>only</b> recommended in the case of a malfunction event or if in service cleaning is required.</li><li>If damage is found to any of the existing element screens the recommended direction is to replace all filter elements with the new part number, although alternatively the damaged elements could be replaced with the existing part number. The new filter elements is <b>not</b> be mixed in with the existing part numbers.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Tables 1 and 2 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts New Eliminator™ Assembly</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK78 CM500)</td><td>3649467/5538155</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540795</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK78 CM2350)</td><td>5538359</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540795</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK60)</td><td>5372017</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540796</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK60 CM2350)</td><td>5372167</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540796</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK45)</td><td>5371886</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540797</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK38/50)</td><td>5538131</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540798</td></tr><tr><td>With Filling Tube</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK38/50 CM2350) With Filling Tube</td><td>5538135</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540798</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Service Parts New Eliminator™ Element Screens</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Filter Element</td><td>3393557</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>3651716</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>New filter element screens are <b>not</b> to be mixed in with the existing part numbers, if new and existing screens are used together this can cause damage to the internals.</p><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The filter element screens are only distinguishable by the pressed supplier part number 1800-03335 for the new screen and 1800-03336 on the old screens.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 3.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td><td>Shop Order</td></tr><tr><td>66305764 (QSK78)</td><td>24 April 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO11656</td></tr><tr><td>33221666 (QSK60)</td><td>19 May 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO10798</td></tr><tr><td>33221557 (QSK60 CM2350)</td><td>27 April 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO11807</td></tr><tr><td>ESN Last</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td><td>Shop Order</td></tr><tr><td>33221517 (QSK45)</td><td>17 April 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO11947</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220022":"<h2>Engine Control Module (ECM) Will Not Communicate or Will Not Accept ECM Calibration Code</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>ROM Booted CM2180 ECM should <b>not</b> attempt to be recovered by recalibrating. See Technical Service Bulletin, INSITE™ Electronic Service Tool Error 5080 - Electronic Control Module (ECM) ROM-Booted or ROM-Booted in a Password Protected State, TSB150120.</p></div></div><p>All models with Engine Control Modules (ECM) except CM420 (B5.9G, B5.9LPG, C8.3G, L10G), ECM B (CELECT™), CM400 (CENTRY™), CM800 (ISBe, ISB four-cylinder), and CM551D (ISB light-duty Chrysler™).</p><p><b>Applicable Engine Control Modules:</b></p><ul><li>CM2150</li><li>CM2180/CM2380</li><li>CM2220</li><li>CM2250</li><li>CM2330</li><li>CM2350/CM2450</li><li>CM2450</li><li>CM2358A</li><li>CM2620</li><li>CM2670</li><li>CM2880</li><li>CM3230</li><li>CM500</li><li>CM550</li><li>CM552</li><li>CM554</li><li>CM556B</li><li>CM558</li><li>CM570</li><li>CM700</li><li>CM850</li><li>CM870</li><li>CM871</li><li>CM875</li><li>CM876</li><li>ECM C</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Some ECMs have been replaced and Cummins Inc. has determined a portion of them were operating properly after being reset and calibrated. Unnecessary replacements of functional ECMs can be avoided by following the ROM boot and calibration procedures listed in this document.</p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>No communication with ECM</li><li>ECM does <b>not</b> accept ECM calibration code</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>ECM calibration was <b>not</b> performed correctly</li><li>Power or communication interruption during ECM calibration</li><li>Incorrect ECM calibration code</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Verify there is no communication with the ECM or that the ECM will <b>not</b> accept a calibration code.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Before replacing the ECM, reset the ECM using the ROM boot process and attempt to recalibrate the ECM.</p><ul><li>Reset the ECM software using the ROM boot procedures listed in Table 1 and verify that INSITE™ shows a similar message as in Figure 1 below.</li><li>Recalibrate the ECM using the ECM calibration procedures listed in Table 1 below.</li><li>For general tool information, including the correct installation configuration and required cables, see the ECM-specific Bench Calibration Base Harness Service Tool Instruction, Bulletin 3377791.</li><li>If the bench calibration base harness or any of the required hardware is <b>not</b> available, follow the technical escalation process.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/19r99820.png\" alt=\"19r99820.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Example of the Error Message From INSITE™ Confirming the ECM Is Rom-Booted and Needs Recalibration.</p><p><b>Associated Publications</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, ECM Associated Publications</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Publication Type</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>ECM ROM Boot</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>See corresponding Service Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module ROM Boot</td><td>019-427</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>See corresponding Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module ROM Boot</td><td>019-427</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Technical Service Bulletin</td><td>TSB150120</td><td>INSITE™ Electronic Service Tool Error 5080 - Electronic Control Module (ECM) ROM-Booted or ROM-Booted in a Password Protected State</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>ECM Calibration</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Service Tool Instruction</td><td>3377791</td><td>ECM Bench Calibration Base Harness</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>See corresponding Service Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module Calibration Code</td><td>019-032</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>See corresponding Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module Calibration Code</td><td>019-032</td><td>19</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220058":"<h2>Valve Guide Wear Caused by Oil and Carbon Buildup from Valve Stem Seal: Black Smoke, Engine Noise, or Lower Power</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 PG CM850</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptoms:</p><ul><li>Excessive black smoke</li><li>Abnormal engine noise</li><li>Low power</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Existing Tier 2 valve stem seal is <b>not</b> intended for the engine operating exhaust port pressures and hence, does <b>not</b> seal the exhaust gases or meter oil per requirement.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Upon inspection would oil/soot mixture found packed in the cylinder head near valve stems and springs and valve stem seals.</li><li>Upon removal of the valve stem guides and valves severe amount of corrosion, carbon deposits and material loss from valve guides is observed.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Replace Tier 2 Valve Stem Seal with Tier 4 Valve Stem Seal and/or Replace entire cylinder head assembly with new part number.</p><ol><li>Remove Suspect Cylinder Head from Engine</li></ol><ul><li>See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 002-004 in Section 2.</li></ul><ol><li>Suspect Cylinder Head Removal Preparation</li></ol><ul><li>Purchase a full set of QST30 cylinder heads. See Parts list in Appendix A1.1.</li><li>Purchase all new parts as specified in parts list and reference reuse list for reworking used good cylinder heads in Appendix A1.2</li></ul><ol><li>Disassemble Suspect Cylinder Head</li></ol><ul><li>Disassemble cylinder head following Procedure 002-004 and 002-020 in Section 2</li><li>Skip removing exhaust and intake valve guides unless reworking good used cylinder head.</li><li>Remove tier 2 valve stem oil seals.</li><li>Skip Clean, Inspect, Leak and Pressure Test sections in 002-004 in Section 2 unless reusing good used cylinder head continue following all procedures.</li></ul><ol><li>Installing Replacement Cylinder Head</li></ol><ul><li>Install replacement cylinder head. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 002-004 in Section 2</li></ul><ol><li>Reworking Suspect Tier 2 Cylinder Head</li></ol><p>Assembly Preparation</p><p>Gather all supplies and tools needed for performing assembly.</p><p>Cummins® Tools Needed Prior to Assembly:</p><ul><li>Valve guide driver, Part Number 6304260</li><li>Valve seal driver, Part Number 3163429</li><li>Valve spring compressor, Part Number ST-448 or Part Number 3163639, or equivalent</li><li>Valve spring stand, Part Number ST-1022</li><li>Compressor plate, Part Number 3375043</li></ul><p>Assembly:</p><ul><li>First few cylinder heads assembled, measure all valve guide lengths, and record the value and compare with guide overall length dimensions located in Appendix B1.</li><li>Install exhaust and intake valve guides using tool when reworking good used cylinder head</li><li>Verify guide installs properly to 21.5 mm by measuring guide exposed difference (see B1.2).</li><li>Install Tier 4 valve stem seals on all exhaust and intake valve guides by using tool, Part Number 3163429.</li><li>Continue assembling cylinder head. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 002-004 and 002-020 in Section 2.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Cylinder head assembly was changed to counter recurring valve stem seal malfunctions in the field.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Cylinder Head</td><td>40686731</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>55936202</td></tr><tr><td>1 All parts have already been installed with new (Tier 4) valve stem seal and valve stem guide pressed to proper depth. 2 New part number in the process of being released.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Service Tools and Training Available</b></p><p>New tool for installing valve stem guide to the proper depth has been released and will be in stock shortly. See Appendix B1 for dimensional print.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37284774</td><td>1 June 2021</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Appendix A1</b></p><p>A1.1 - New Cylinder Head Parts List</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, New Cylinder Head Parts List</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Head Quantity</td><td>Minimum Purchase Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Tier (T) 2 Cylinder Head Assembly</td><td>5593620</td><td>1</td><td>&gt;12</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Rework Parts List</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Based on Condition denotes following clean, inspection, and leak test procedures found on Procedure 002-004 and 002-020 in Section 2.</p></div></div><p>Reference assembly layout continued in Appendix A2 for item number references.</p><p>A1.2 - Rework Parts List</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Rework Parts List</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number in Figure 1</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Head Quantity</td><td>Reuse/Replace</td><td>Comments</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>T2 Cylinder Head Assembly</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4068673\">4068673</a></td><td>1</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>T2 Cylinder Head Rebuild Kit</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5579210\">5579210</a></td><td>1</td><td>Purchase</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>T4 Valve Stem Seal</td><td>2897022</td><td>4</td><td>Purchase</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Valve Collets</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092344\">3092344</a></td><td>8</td><td>Replace</td><td>Discard and use new collets</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Exhaust Valve Kit -1-Valve, 2-Collets</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3804867\">3804867</a></td><td>2</td><td>Replace -Valve stems and collets</td><td>Use new valve stems and collets</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Intake Valve Kit -1-Valve, 2-Collets</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3804866\">3804866</a></td><td>2</td><td>Replace -Valve stems and collets</td><td>Use new valve stems and collets</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>T2 Valve Stem Seal</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092608\">3092608</a></td><td>4</td><td>Replace</td><td>Discard and use T4 seals</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Valve Stem Guide</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092014\">3092014</a></td><td>4</td><td>Replace</td><td>Use new valve guides</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Intake Valve Seat (Valve Insert)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3094159\">3094159</a></td><td>2</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Exhaust Valve Seat (Valve Insert)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092461\">3092461</a></td><td>2</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Valve Spring Retainer</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092510\">3092510</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Large Spring</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092508\">3092508</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Small Spring</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092509\">3092509</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Valve Spring Guide</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092511\">3092511</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Appendix A2</b></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00141.png\" alt=\"02r00141.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, QST30 CM552 Cylinder Head Layout.</p><p><b>Appendix B1</b></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00142.png\" alt=\"02r00142.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Valve Guide OAL: 72.5 0/-0.5 mm.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00143.png\" alt=\"02r00143.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Valve Guide Install Depth Difference.</p><p>Previous Valve Guide Install Depth Difference 23 mm +/-0.2 mm</p><p>New Valve Guide Install Depth Difference 21.5 mm +/-0.2 mm</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220121":"<h2>New Heat Shield Part Number</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>Existing heat shields have been superseded with new designs of heat shields.</li><li>The new designs have driven part number changes that are communicated in Table 1 below.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Improvements have been made on the new designs to increase thermal containment efficiency and reduce the space claim of the heat shields.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>New part numbers <b>must</b> be fitted as a pair. See Part Compatibility section below for more information.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Shield, Heat</td><td>3643681</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542292</td></tr><tr><td>Shield, Heat</td><td>3643682</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542293</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>Do <b>not</b> mix existing and new part numbers.</li><li>Example: 5542292 is designed to fit with 5542293 and will <b>not</b> fit with 3643682.</li><li>The new parts together give full backwards compatibility to the overall heat shield assembly.</li></ul><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33224989</td><td>1 June 2021</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220196":"<h2>Electric Starting Motor Changes</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KV38 (All Versions)</li><li>KV50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK38 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new starting motor is available.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Production has ceased on all MS7 and M125 starting motor and the M128 starting motor is the replacement.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Parts and Compatibility</p><ul><li>If using a single (quantity 1) MS7 or M125 starting motor on an engine. The M128 starting motor is a direct replacement.</li><li>If using dual (quantity 2) MS7 or M125 starting motor on an engine. Then both starting motors <b>must</b> be replaced with M128 starting motor.</li><li>If using triple (quantity 3) starting motor. All 3 starting motor <b>must</b> be replaced with M128 starting motor.</li><li>Table 3 shows starting motor part number supersession and all new part numbers.</li><li>New brackets have been released to mount the magnetic switches to the M128 starting motor and are listed in Table 3.</li></ul><p>Installation</p><ul><li>The orientation of the starting motor when fitting to the flywheel housing is dictated by the flat side of the mounting flange. The flat side of the mounting flange is the side that <b>must</b> be oriented to the side face of the cylinder block for correct starting motor orientation. Figure 1 shows the flat face of the starting motor mounting flange.</li><li>The orientation of the starting motor will also dictate the magnetic switch bracket to be used.</li><li>Figure 2 shows the correct mounting bracket depending on starting motor orientation.</li><li>Table 2 communicates the correct magnetic switch bracket part number.</li><li>Figure 3 shows the exploded starting motor assembly detailing the magnetic switch and is applicable for both orientations of mounting bracket and single/dual magnetic switch assemblies.</li><li>A torque table (Table 2) is available for updated joints on the assemblies.</li><li>Figure 4 shows the wiring terminals that <b>must</b> be used to wire the starting motor correctly. The T50C (Ignition ‘S&#x27;) terminal <b>must</b> be balanced using 16AWG wire between both T50C (Ignition ‘S&#x27;) starting motor terminals.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00211.png\" alt=\"13r00211.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Arrow Indicating the Flat Side of the Starting motor Mounting Flange.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00212.png\" alt=\"13r00212.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Magnetic Switch Orientations. Left: QSK. Right: KV.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Magnetic Switch Bracket Part Numbers</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Engine</td><td>Engine bank</td><td>Dual</td><td>Single</td></tr><tr><td>KV</td><td>Left</td><td>6377449</td><td>6377447</td></tr><tr><td>Right</td><td>6377448</td><td>6377446</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>QSK</td><td>Left</td><td>6377445</td><td>6377435</td></tr><tr><td>Right</td><td>6377445</td><td>6377435</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00213.png\" alt=\"13r00213.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Magnetic Switch Exploded Diagram.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Torque</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Joint Description</td><td>Torque (Nm)</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3913638\">3913638</a></td><td>Mounting Bracket</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>M5 starting motor Ignition (S/T50C) Terminal</td><td>2 to 2.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00214.png\" alt=\"13r00214.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Starting Motor Terminal Identification. Left: QSK. Right: KV.</p><ol><li>T50C (Ignition ‘S&#x27;) Terminal – <b>Must</b> be balanced using 16AWG wire.</li><li>T30 (B+) Terminal – Outboard most terminal.</li><li>T31 (GND) Terminal – Longer of the two Terminals.</li></ol><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 3 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>KV / QSK</td><td>Manufacturer Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>MOTOR, STARTING</td><td>QSK</td><td>(MS7)</td><td>3632273</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541784</td></tr><tr><td>3632274</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541783</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>KV</td><td>(MS7)</td><td>3632273</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542145</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3632274</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538989</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>QSK</td><td>(M125)</td><td>3647165</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541784</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3647166</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541783</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>KV</td><td>(M125)</td><td>3647165</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542145</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3647166</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538989</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>(M128)</td><td>5541783</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5541784</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5538989</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5542145</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>BRACKET, MAGNETIC SWITCH</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6377449</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6377448</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377445</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377445</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377447</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377446</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377435</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377435</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>SCREW, CAP</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3913638\">3913638</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>Full compatibility when used in single starting motor applications.</li><li>No compatibility when used in dual or triple starting motor applications.</li><li>The obsolete MS7 and M125 are hard engagement starting motor. The M128 starting motor is a soft engagement starting motor. Use of hard and soft engagement starting motors together is <b>not</b> allowed.</li></ul><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 4.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33228264</td><td>29 April 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220212":"<h2>Industrial Low Temperature Aftercooler (LTA) Water Pump Repair Kits</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T103C</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>This document announces new LTA water pump repair kit part numbers.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Repair Kits for LTA Water Pump, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5409320\">5409320</a></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Part Name</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6389213\">6389213</a></td><td>KIT,WATER PUMP REPAIR (MINOR)</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6388354\">6388354</a></td><td>KIT,WATER PUMP REPAIR (MAJOR)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><ul><li>Table 2 documents a complete parts list for both repair kits and Figure 1 shows part location.</li><li>Some parts within the LTA water pump, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5409320\">5409320</a>, are available for individual purchase. Reference Table 3 for a complete list.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Parts List for Repair Kit, Part Numbers <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6389213\">6389213</a> and <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6388354\">6388354</a></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number in Figure 1</td><td>Name</td><td>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6389213\">6389213</a></td><td>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6388354\">6388354</a></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>LIPSEAL</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>DEEP GROVE BALL BEARING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>INTERNAL BEVELLED CIRCLIP</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>O RING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>PUMP SHAFT</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>BALL BEARING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>SHIM WASHER</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>LIPSEAL</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>O RING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>O RING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>SHAFT SLEEVE</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>MECHANICAL SEAL</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>CHEVRON IMPELLER</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>ORING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>V CLAMP</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>GASKET</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Purchasable Parts Within LTA Water Pump</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>SCREW,SOCKET HEAD CAP</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025372\">4025372</a></td></tr><tr><td>WASHER, SPRING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025373\">4025373</a></td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025374\">4025374</a></td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025376\">4025376</a></td></tr><tr><td>CLAMP, V BAND</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025377\">4025377</a></td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, WATER PUMP</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025378\">4025378</a></td></tr><tr><td>SCREW,SOCKET HEAD CAP</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5416040\">5416040</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08r00578.png\" alt=\"08r00578.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, LTA Water Pump.</p><p><b>Associated Publications</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Associated Publications</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM552</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a></td><td>Low-Temperature (LTA) Water Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 008-109</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM2350 T101</td><td>5467179</td><td>Low-Temperature (LTA) Water Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 008-109</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM2350 T103C</td><td>6309062</td><td>Low-Temperature (LTA) Water Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 008-109</td><td>8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230019":"<h2>New ReCon® Aftertreatment NOx Sensors Causing Fault Code 3232 or 2771</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>All products utilizing an aftertreatment NOx sensor</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>After installation of a “new” ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment intake NOx sensor, Fault Code 3232 is active.</li><li>After installation of a “new” ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment outlet NOx sensor, Fault Code 2771 is active.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>An aftertreatment NOx sensor quality issue was identified in a limited number of sensors manufactured before 19 January 2023.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Fault Code 3232 or 2771 is active at key-on immediately after installing a “new” ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment NOx sensor. This issue <b>only</b> affects ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment NOx sensors (Part numbers are listed in Table 1) that have gone through the remanufacturing process. These aftertreatment NOx sensors can be identified by the inline connector that is present in the aftertreatment NOx sensor probe wiring as shown in Figure 1.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Affected ReCon® Aftertreatment NOx Sensor Part Numbers</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Sensor Description</td></tr><tr><td>4326862RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326865RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326867RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326870RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326873RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4327153RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>3687930RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>3688978RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>5289662RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>5295473RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326861RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326863RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326864RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326866RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326868RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326869RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326871RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326872RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326874RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>5419105RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/19r99901.png\" alt=\"19r99901.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Aftertreatment NOx Sensor Probe Inline Connector.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Replace the aftertreatment NOx sensor. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 019-451 or 019-463 in Section 19.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230092":"<h2>Fan Clutch Oil Supply Manifold Solenoid Re-orientation</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li></ul><p><b>Issue</b></p><p>Fan clutch oil solenoid coil malfunction causes permanent cooling fan engagement. Fault codes 245 and 2377 become active. Permanent full fan speed can cause over-cooling and excessive noise.</p><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Engine cooling fan runs constantly</li><li>Fault codes 245 and 2377 become active</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>A new fan clutch oil manifold has been designed to improve oil cooling on the oil control solenoid coil. This fan clutch oil manifold is released in a parts kit (part number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6468466\">6468466</a>). The parts kit contains all the required components to upfit the new manifold and separately available solenoid (part number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3641305\">3641305</a>) to the engine.</p><p>Existing design oil manifold:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00150.png\" alt=\"16r00150.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Existing Oil Manifold Installation.</p><p>New design oil manifold:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00151.png\" alt=\"16r00151.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, New Design Oil Manifold Installation.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>The new design oil manifold rotates the solenoid mounting location by 180°. In this orientation, cooling oil is gravity fed to the solenoid coil, reducing coil temperature and therefore the risk of malfunction.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Order upfit kit, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6468466\">6468466</a> and solenoid valve, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3641305\">3641305</a>, before attempting to carry out this repair. Check part quantities delivered in the parts kit, as per Table 1, Service Parts. Remove the five male – male unions/ adapters from the existing manifold. Re-use these unions/ connectors. O-rings will be provided in the parts kit.</p><p>To replace the fan clutch oil control manifold and solenoid, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 007-125 in Section 7.</p><ul><li>GTA38, K38, K50 QSK38 and QSK50 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108 Service Manual, Bulletin 4332823</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130 Service Manual, Bulletin 5504180</li></ul><p>Following installation of the fan clutch oil control manifold and solenoid, the solenoid wiring harness <b>must</b> be re-routed to allow connector alignment with the new solenoid position. This process differs for Tier 2 and Tier 4 engines. Verify the route required and re-route as shown in Figure 5.</p><p>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS Engines:</p><ul><li>Remove the solenoid harness from the shared P-clip, holding the solenoid harness and the engine harness, to the rocker cover (circled yellow). Cut the cable tie securing the solenoid harness to the existing engine harness. Figure 3 shows the existing route for Tier 2 engines.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00152.png\" alt=\"16r00152.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Tier 2 Engine Wiring Harness Routing.</p><p>QSK50 CM2350 K108/ K130 Engines:</p><ul><li>Figure 4 shows the exisiting Tier 4 solenoid harness route, which has an engine harness P-clipped to it on the rocker cover. Remove the solenoid harness from the engine harness P-clip.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00153.png\" alt=\"16r00153.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Tier 4 Engine Wiring Harness Route.</p><p>All Engines:</p><ul><li>Secure existing Tier 2 and Tier 4 non-solenoid harnesses as per the original installation.</li><li>Re-route the solenoid harness to the new solenoid position. Figure 6 shows the required new route using two P-clips (part number 68152A) on existing gear cover capscrew locations across both Tier 2 and Tier 4 engines.</li><li>Remove two gear cover capscrews and install the solenoid harness through the P-clips, vertically upwards as shown.</li><li>Install the capscrews.</li></ul><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>75 n•m [55 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00154.png\" alt=\"16r00154.png\"></figure><p>Figure 5, New Solenoid Harness Routing.</p><ul><li>Connect the wiring harness to the solenoid plug. Verify a positive “click” as the connector fork slots over the plug and locks in position to avoid backing out.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available in kit form. See Table 1 for part numbers contained within the parts kit.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td><td>Part Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>MANIFOLD, OIL</td><td>3640889</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6414133\">6414133</a></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>SCREW, SOCKET HEAD CAP</td><td>S143B</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>WASHER, LOCK</td><td>S604</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>GASKET, CORROSION RESISTOR</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3650106\">3650106</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>PLUG, PIPE</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3008466\">3008466</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>PLUG, THREADED</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3046202\">3046202</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>PLUG, THREADED</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3037536\">3037536</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>CLIP</td><td>68152A</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3348871\">3348871</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3627695\">3627695</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089240\">3089240</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3630740\">3630740</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3922794\">3922794</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3037537\">3037537</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>The new oil manifold is common across the QSK50 Tier 2 and Tier 4, as was the existing design. New and existing oil manifolds are backwards compatible. The new manifold is advised for expected fan solenoid operation and service life.</p><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The new oil manifold is different in shape to the existing part, allowing the oil control solenoid to sit upside down. The new part number is stamped in the location shown in Figure 6 The existing part number is stamped exactly 180° opposite to this on the bottom face. Both part numbers are stamped on the face opposite to the solenoid when viewed as installed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00155.png\" alt=\"16r00155.png\"></figure><p>Figure 6, Part Number Stamping Location on New Fan Clutch Oil Manifold.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Obsolete part numbers in stock are <b>not</b> to be used.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33231741</td><td>8 April 2023</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Fan Clutch Lubricating Oil Manifold</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-125\">Refer to Procedure 007-125</a></td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Fan Clutch Lubricating Oil Manifold</td><td>Refer to Procedure 007-125</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K108</td><td>4332823</td><td>Fan Clutch Lubricating Oil Manifold</td><td>Refer to Procedure 007-125</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230094":"<h2>New Engine Paint</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>K38 (All Versions)</li><li>K50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK38 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK45 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK78 (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>Switching from oil-based paint to a new water-based paint which is now being used on all Daventry Engine Plant manufactured engines. The change was effective on the 03 January 2023.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Paint defects such as peeling, cracking, and fading during application have been observed. The new water-based paint has better thermal capabilities, has performed better over extended use in applications, and is more environmentally friendly.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>No new aftermarket touch-up water-based paint is available. Existing aftermarket paint is oil-based and there may be visual differences in shade and finish. Reference Table 1 for the applicable engineering standards (use Cummins® Engineering Standards and RAL codes to request water-based touch-up paint from an external supplier).</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Paint Description and Color Standard</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Color Description</td><td>Color standard</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS BEIGE, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS RED, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS MARINE GRAY, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS BLACK, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS ONAN GREEN, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS CLOUD WHITE, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>KOMATSU YELLOW</td><td>*</td></tr><tr><td>TRAFFIC GRAY</td><td>RAL7043</td></tr><tr><td>BASALT GRAY</td><td>RAL7012</td></tr><tr><td>*Komatsu engineering standard</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Because of the difference in viscosity between water-based and aerosol oil-based paints, casting imperfections will be more noticeable.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paint color</td><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>Beige</td><td>33230671</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Red</td><td>33230409</td><td>1 April 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>33230678</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Marine Grey</td><td>33229985</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Komatsu Yellow</td><td>33230291</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td>33230443</td><td>1 April 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Basalt (Terex) Grey</td><td>33230958</td><td>2 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Liebherr (Traffic) Grey</td><td>33230695</td><td>1 May 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Cloud White</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230096":"<h2>ECM Reset and Communication Issue Troubleshooting</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>ECM <b>not</b> able to communicate upon engine key on</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>ECM issue</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Use the following steps to diagnose ECM:</p><ol><li>With Calterm® (preferred) or INSITE™ electronic service tool and a datalink adapter hooked up to a suspect ECM.</li><li>Set battery voltage at 26V to 28V.</li><li>Key on and verify if the ECM will communicate. Key off. Repeat a few times.</li><li>If the ECM will <b>not</b> communicate key off and reduce battery voltage to 12V to 14V.</li><li>Key on and see if the ECM will communicate.</li><li>If the ECM will <b>not</b> communicate at the higher voltage but does at the lower voltage, the ECM likely has the reset issue.</li><li>A malfunctioning ECM has been detected when keyed on at a higher voltage and <b>not</b> communicating will be continuously resetting.</li></ol><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If using Calterm® and can communicate, check the reset parameter NRESETSE and see how many resets have occurred. A normally functioning module should <b>not</b> have any or <b>only</b> a few resets. Improper shutdowns (removing battery voltage in less than 30 sec after keying off) will increment the reset count each occurrence. The longer the ECM is left on and <b>not</b> communicating the higher the reset count will be when the ECM does communicate at the lower voltage. The reset count can increment by hundreds in just a few minutes.</p></div></div><p>Fault codes that can be seen for suspect ECMs are listed below:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Fault Codes</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fault Code</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>285</td><td>SAE J1939 Multiplexing PGN Timeout Error - Abnormal Update Rate</td></tr><tr><td>346</td><td>Engine Control Module Warning Software error - Bad intelligent device or component</td></tr><tr><td>426</td><td>SAE J1939 Datalink - Data Erratic, Intermittent, or Incorrect; J1939 Network #1 - Data erratic, intermittent or incorrect</td></tr><tr><td>434</td><td>Power Lost With Ignition On - Data Erratic, Intermittent, or Incorrect</td></tr><tr><td>1117</td><td>Power Lost With Ignition On - Data Erratic, Intermittent, or Incorrect</td></tr><tr><td>1135</td><td>SAE J1939 Data Link 2 Engine Network - Abnormal Update Rate</td></tr><tr><td>2727</td><td>SAE J1939 Data Link 2 Engine Network - Abnormal Update Rate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Replace malfunctioning ECM. See QST30 Industrial Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666214\">3666214</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/87-019-031\">Refer to Procedure 019-031 in Section 19.</a></p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230138":"<h2>New Coolant Thermostat</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 K132M</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS M</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K149M</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Engine shut down due to Fault Code 146 Engine coolant temperature is above the engine protection warning limit.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Coolant thermostats are damaged or stuck closed.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Inspect coolant thermostats for damage. If coolant thermostats are found to be damaged or stuck closed, follow instructions in resolution section.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If coolant thermostats are found to be damaged or stuck closed, replace with new part number in Table 1.</li><li>For coolant thermostat removal and installation instructions, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 008-013 in Section 8.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Coolant Thermostat</td><td>3629205</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5538724\">5538724</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230167":"<h2>Missing Rocker Lever Rivets</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KTAA19 K131G</li><li>KTAA38 K134G</li><li>KTA50 K148G</li><li>QSK19 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK19 CM2150 K139M</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K114</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K144G</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K167C</li><li>QSK19 CM2450 K155G</li><li>QSK38 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K142M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K145G</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K140M</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS G</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K146G</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K163C</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Missing rivet at rocker lever cross drilling</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Improper fit between rocker lever drilling inside diameter and rivet outside diameter</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Verification is visual <b>only</b> and does <b>not</b> have to be proactive. During planned maintenance of the overhead, remove rocker lever cover and inspect rocker levers.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If a missing rivet is verified, options for replacement are available. The original rivet can be installed in existing rocker lever or rocker lever assembly can be replaced with a new part number with an improved plug. Plug design changed from a rivet to ball plug style. See Table 1 for part number details.</li><li>Replacement of the rivet does <b>not</b> have to be proactive as missing rivet has no impact on oil pressure. Replacement can occur during a planned engine rebuild.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</li><li>Rocker lever assembly, top level, and kit part numbers did <b>not</b> change. Part numbers within the kits and assemblies did change. See Table 1 for details.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Lever, Rocker</td><td>3053476</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6377069\">6377069</a></td></tr><tr><td>Lever, Rocker</td><td>3053477</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6377067\">6377067</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33233267</td><td>August 2023</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37291483</td><td>August 2023</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230183":"<h2>New Rear Crankshaft Seal Part Number</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>K38 (All versions)</li><li>K50 (All versions)</li><li>QSK38 (All versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A new rear crankshaft seal has been released which has a larger outer diameter 214.12 mm [ 8.429 in ] than the existing version 206.38 mm [ 8.125 in ].</li><li>Associated flywheel housings have also had part number changes to accommodate the larger diameter crankshaft seal.</li><li>Service Tooling kit part numbers have been updated to accommodate the new design rear crankshaft seal.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>The new design of crankshaft seal and service tooling improves installation errors, lube oil sealing, and component longevity.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3176637</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374479</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>4917572</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5374489\">5374489</a></td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3414620</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374494</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>4978338</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374498</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3628045</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374483</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3176637</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374479</td></tr><tr><td>Seal, Crank</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3642365\">3642365</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>4396606</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>No backwards compatibility exists between part numbers in Table 1.</li><li>Table 2 shows the part compatibility matrix.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Part Compatibility Matrix</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flywheel Housing</td><td>New Crankshaft Seal, Part Number 4396606 (214.12 mm [ 8.429 in ] Diameter) Compatibility</td><td>Existing Crankshaft Seal, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3642365\">3642365</a> (206.38 mm [ 8.125 in ] Diameter) Compatibility</td></tr><tr><td>Flywheel Housing (214.12 mm [ 8.429 in ] Bore)</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Flywheel Housing (206.38 mm [ 8.125 in ] Bore)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><ul><li>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</li><li>The existing crankshaft seal will remain available as the existing crankshaft seal is used on other engine configurations.</li></ul><p><b>Service Tools and Training Available</b></p><p>The service tool kit has been updated to include parts that aid the installation of both existing and new crankshaft seals.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. Reference Table 3.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33224419</td><td>31 March 2021</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240023":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve: External Fuel Leak</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>External fuel leak from fuel shutoff valve shield</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Fuel shutoff valve shield malfunction</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>The fuel shutoff valve is a serviceable component. This malfunction can be repaired by replacing the fuel shutoff valve shield. See QST30 Service Manual. Refer to Procedure 005-043 in Section 5 for replacement procedure.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>For customers experiencing frequent external fuel leaks from the fuel shutoff shield, customers should work with their local distributor or sales and services team to determine an appropriate interval to routinely replace both shutoff valve shields during planned downtime events.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>See QST30 Service Manual. Refer to Procedure 005-043 in Section 5 for instructions on fuel shutoff valve disassembly and assembly. Figure 1 below identifies the fuel shutoff valve shield in the overall assembly.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00361.png\" alt=\"05r00361.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Exploded View of Fuel Shutoff Valve Assembly.</p><ol><li>Shutoff valve shield, Part Number 129839</li><li>Rectangular ring seal, Part Number 129888</li><li>Rectangular ring seal, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4975411\">4975411</a></li></ol><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>See Table 1 for minimum part numbers that will be needed at time of Shutoff Valve Shield replacement.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Minimum Parts for Shutoff Valve Shield Maintenance Replacement (Per Engine)</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Shutoff Valve Shield</td><td>129839</td><td>2 (1 per fuel shutoff valve)</td></tr><tr><td>Rectangular Ring Seal</td><td>129888</td><td>2 (1 per fuel shutoff valve)</td></tr><tr><td>Rectangular Ring Seal</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4975411\">4975411</a></td><td>2 (1 per fuel shutoff valve)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240059":"<h2>Repair Strategy for Fuel Lift Pump Relay and Wiring Harness: Fault Codes 583, 2265, 2266</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Burned or malfunctioning fuel lift pump relay.</li><li>Engine Difficult to Start or Will Not Start (No Exhaust Smoke)</li><li>Potential fault codes:</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>After following the published troubleshooting manual procedures, if a fuel lift pump relay is found to be dysfunctional, inspect the fuel lift pump relay and wiring harness connector for signs of heat damage, such as melted plastic.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00365.png\" alt=\"05r00365.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Examples of Burned Fuel Lift Pump Relay and Wiring Harness Connector.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>If any signs of heat damage are present on either the fuel lift pump relay and/or the wiring harness connector, replace both the fuel lift pump relay and wiring harness.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240105":"<h2>New Pre-Lube Pressure Switch</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T103C</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Engine will <b>not</b> start</li><li>Lubricating oil leaking through the pre-lube pressure switch</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Engine <b>not</b> starting- soldering the wires to the internal electrical contacts of the pre lube pressure switch.</li><li>Oil leaks- O-ring and diaphragm hardening because of unsuitable materials.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Visually inspect the pre-lube pressure switch to identify the lubricating oil leak through the vent hole on the switch.</li><li>Use multimeter to measure resistance of pre-lube switch when pressure is applied. Variable resistance or infinite resistance indicates poor wire connection.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If customer is experiencing multiple pre-lube pressure switch malfunctions, replace malfunctioning switch with new pre-lube pressure switch.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>For pre lube pressure switch removal and installation instructions, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 007-086 in Section 7.</li><li>Before connecting the pre-lube switch harness to the engine harness, use quick-dry electrical contact cleaner, Part Number 3824510, to remove all dirt and moisture from the pre-lube switch connector and the engine wiring harness connector.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-lube Pressure Switch</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4924925\">4924925</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6396482</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Reallocate stock of the existing pre-lube pressure switch, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4924925\">4924925</a>, to QSK50 and QSK60 engines.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Engine Model</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37296673</td><td>QST30 CM552</td><td>7 April 2025</td><td>SEP</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240127":"<h2>New HE800 Turbocharger Bearing Housing Design</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK45 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>New HE800 turbochargers are being released with a new design of bearing housing which contains a coolant jacket for coolant cooling. This bearing housing has been designed so that the turbocharger can be used both wet and dry depending on if the engine has coolant plumbing already built in.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>If the turbocharger is going to be run dry, there will be no risk made by debris getting into the turbocharger as the coolant channel is completely sealed off from the turbocharger. Any debris that will enter the turbocharger will sit in a coolant channel. Do <b>not</b> block off any coolant ports as this could cause a heat trap and result in an overheated turbocharger.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240153":"<h2>New Turbocharger Compressor Wheel for Single Stage HE800 Mining Engines: Turbocharger Compressor Wheel Splitting</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Issue</b></p><p>Aluminium HE800 Turbocharger compressor wheels used in single stage mining applications have malfunctioned because of fatigue. The compressor wheel splits through the wheel hub centre line and damages the compressor cover, turbine wheel and shaft. Malfunctions can spread debris into the aftercoolers on the affected bank.</p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Reports of low power and black smoke from the affected bank</li><li>Turbocharger compressor wheel is found split through the hub center line</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>The existing turbocharger compressor wheel material can experience crack initiation and fatigue propagation under certain duty cycle loads.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Verify low power, black smoke from the affected bank, and low intake manifold pressure.</li><li>Turbocharger compressor inspection is required to verify the failure mode. The turbocharger compressor wheel will be split in two or three pieces (Figure 1).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02223.png\" alt=\"17r02223.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Turbocharger Compressor Wheel Split Malfunction.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>Turbochargers <b>must</b> be replaced with new parts when the split compressor wheel failure mode has been verified. Remove aftercooler covers and clean or replace aftercoolers which are damaged or contaminated with compressor wheel debris.</li><li>In the event of a turbocharger malfunction, the use of the new turbocharger part number is recommended. See Table 1.</li><li>A new compressor wheel material has been used to improve HE800 robustness in single stage turbocharged mine haul truck and excavator applications. New turbocharger part numbers have been released for production and service. New turbochargers retain every feature of the previous part numbers, aside from the improved compressor wheel material.</li><li>The new compressor wheel material has improved capability to resist increased stress at the compressor wheel to oil slinger interface. Increased stresses have been attributed to turbocharger resonance under certain running conditions.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>If a turbocharger malfunction has been verified, remove aftercooler covers to inspect for debris. Clean the aftercooler housing and core if required. Install the aftercooler and covers. See QSK50 CM2150 MCRS Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-010-008-tr\">Refer to Procedure 010-008</a> in Section 10.</li><li>Replace turbocharger. See QSK50 CM2150 MCRS Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-010-033-tr\">Refer to Procedure 010-033</a> in Section 19.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</li><li>Turbocharger availability for service will be in the form turbocharger kits <b>only</b>. The kit will include the new turbocharger, as well as the required gaskets and seals to replace one turbocharger.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>KIT, TURBOCHARGER (Left Bank)</td><td>4956149</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6524218</td></tr><tr><td>KIT, TURBOCHARGER (Right Bank)</td><td>4956148</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6524187</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>Turbocharger dataplates mounted to the compressor cover display part number details. For production units, <b>not</b> previously replaced, customer numbers (shown as “Cust” on the dataplate) can be used to identify turbocharger kit part numbers. For previously replaced turbochargers, a turbocharger kit part number will be shown on the turbocharger dataplate. See Table 2 for details.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Customer Number to Kit Part Number Guide</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>Dataplate Customer Number</td><td>Related Turbocharger Kit</td></tr><tr><td>Existing Parts</td><td>4041284</td><td>4956149 (Left Bank)</td></tr><tr><td>4041286</td><td>4956148 (Right Bank)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>New Parts</td><td>6524217</td><td>6524218 (Left Bank)</td></tr><tr><td>6524129</td><td>6524187 (Right Bank)</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Both new part numbers are implemented for production and were built into the ESN First. See Table 3.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33236236</td><td>8 July 2024</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240188":"<h2>New Electric Fuel Lift Pump System</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM850</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new electric fuel lift pump system has been released for the product affected. This change includes a new supplier for the fuel lift pumps, an increase from two to three fuel lift pumps per engine, and new relevant hardware. See Table 3. These new fuel lift pumps are added to the Operation and Maintenance Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666134\">3666134</a>, as maintenance items.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Select the Lift Pump Base Service Kit, Filter Bracket, and Service Wiring Harness from Table 1 needed for the relevant application and configuration. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 005-045 in Section 5, for removal, clean and inspect, and install instructions for the fuel lift pumps. Reference procedure 006-017 in section 6 of the corresponding service manual for removal, clean and inspect and install instructions for the fuel filter head. For new conversions, reference procedure 006-017 in Section 6 of the corresponding service manual for the removal of the old hardware. Reference both procedures 005-045 and 006-017 in Sections 5 and 6 of the corresponding service manual for the installation of the new hardware.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Kits/Parts Needed for Conversion from Two-Pump to Three-Pump System</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application/Configuration</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Base Kit</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>All</td><td>Fuel Lift Pump Base Service Kit</td><td>6563609</td></tr><tr><td>Filter Bracket</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Engines built with one of the options: FF 5079 FF 5080 FF 5086 FF 5088 FF 5105 FF 5107 FF 5108</td><td>Filter Bracket (See Figure 1 in Part Identification section)</td><td>6426058</td></tr><tr><td>Engines built with one of the options: FF 5081 FF 5106</td><td>Filter Bracket with Air Plumbing Support (See Figure 2 in Part Identification section)</td><td>6426729</td></tr><tr><td>Service Wiring Harness</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>PowerGen (12V System)</td><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PG 12V to 24V)</td><td>6470469</td></tr><tr><td>PowerGen (24V System)</td><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PG 24V)</td><td>6470470</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial (12V or 24V System)</td><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – IND)</td><td>6474493</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Kits Needed for <b>Fuel Pump Head Reassembly (No Malfunctioning Fuel Lift Pumps)</b></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Consumables Kit (3 sets of isolators and seals)</td><td>6563611</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Kit Needed for Routine Maintenance and Malfunction Replacements</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Replacement Kit (Consumables Kit + 3 Fuel Lift Pumps)</td><td>6563610</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For field repairs, all three fuel lift pumps <b>must</b> be replaced if one or more fuel lift pump malfunctions.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 4 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Lift Pump</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483862\">5483862</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6413638</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Filter Head</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483701\">5483701</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6425066</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Pump Head</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483705\">5483705</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6425068</td></tr><tr><td>Vibration Isolator</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4067902\">4067902</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6425064</td></tr><tr><td>Filter Bracket</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4295451\">4295451</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6426058</td></tr><tr><td>Filter Bracket with Air Plumbing Support</td><td>4975740</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6426729</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PowerGen 12V to 24V)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6470469</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PowerGen 24V)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6470470</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – Industrial)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6474493</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Pump Kit</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5593523\">5593523</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>See Table 1</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Base Conversion Kit</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6563609</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Consumables Kit (3 sets of isolators and seals)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6563611</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 5.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 5, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application</td><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>PowerGen</td><td>37295184</td><td>4 October 2024</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial</td><td>37295256</td><td>14 October 2024</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th><b>Table 5, Publications Affected</b></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Manual Type</b></td><td><b>Engine</b></td><td><b>Bulletin Number</b></td><td><b>Procedure Title</b></td><td><b>Procedure</b></td><td><b>Section</b></td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a></td><td>Fuel Lift Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 005-045</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Operations and Maintenance Manual</td><td>QST30</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666134\">3666134</a></td><td>Maintenance Schedule</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/57-102-002-om\">Refer to Procedure 102-002</a></td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Operations and Maintenance Manual</td><td>QST30</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666134\">3666134</a></td><td>Fuel Lift Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 005-045</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10r00495.png\" alt=\"10r00495.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Filter Bracket, Part Number 6426058.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10r00496.png\" alt=\"10r00496.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Filter Bracket with Air Plumbing Support, Part Number 6426729.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05m00182.png\" alt=\"05m00182.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Comparison of Installed Two-Pump (Left) and Three-Pump (Right) Systems.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250017":"<h2>New Accessory Drive Seal: Engine Lubricating Oil Leak</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KT38</li><li>KTA38</li><li>KTA50</li><li>KTTA38</li><li>KTTA50</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K106</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K107</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li><li>QSK50 CM500</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A new accessory drive seal with a revised design has been released.</li><li>The new accessory drive seal uses a single piece construction.</li><li>To install the new accessory drive seal, a new service tool has been released.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>The existing accessory drive seal can leak engine lubricating oil.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>All existing pulley part numbers have been obsoleted and superseded by new pulley part numbers.</li><li>New pulleys are sold without a wear sleeve fitted to the pulley shaft, allowing the use of the new accessory drive seal.</li><li>A new pulley <b>must</b> be used when installing the new accessory drive seal. A new accessory drive seal installer part number is available and listed in Table 1.</li><li><b>Never</b> remove the wear sleeve from existing pulleys because of the risk of introducing an engine lubricating oil leak path.</li><li>New pulley part numbers are shown in Table 1, allowing cross reference from the existing pulley part numbers.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</li><li>Existing part numbers will remain available for service on the KV19 and QSK19 engines <b>only</b>.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>ACCESSORY DRIVE SEAL</td><td>3628895</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5694571\">5694571</a></td></tr><tr><td>ACCESSORY DRIVE SEAL</td><td>3628894</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5694571\">5694571</a></td></tr><tr><td>ACCESSORY DRIVE SEAL INSTALLER</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5573466</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3015546</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3628938</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3018761</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3015321\">3015321</a></td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3028587</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3177006</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3045970</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3628937\">3628937</a></td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3179943</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3628940</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3179944</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3628939</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3631843</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3631844</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3645612</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3645579</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ALTERNATOR</td><td>3880348</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>3875798</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>5538240</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538239</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Service Tools and Training Available</b></p><p>A new accessory drive seal installer part number is available and listed in Table 1.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33227622</td><td>21 February 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38/50</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38 CM2150 K106</td><td>4332768</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2150 K107</td><td>4332773</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K108</td><td>4332823</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Gear Cover, Front</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-031</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</td><td>5613395</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</td><td>5613395</td><td>Gear Cover, Front</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-031</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250112":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Leaks from Turbocharger Compressor Outlet</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>External lubricating oil leak from turbocharger compressor outlet and/or air crossover inlet</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>With prolonged operation in low-load and low-speed conditions, lubricating oil can accumulate in the turbocharger bearing housing and leak past the turbocharger compressor seal, leaking externally at the air crossover hose connection between the turbocharger compressor outlet and air crossover inlet.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Visually inspect the turbocharger compressor outlet, air crossover inlet and air crossover hose for signs of an external lubricating oil leak.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Fast-setting RTV is to be applied to the hose connections at the turbocharger compressor outlet and air crossover inlet. Reference Service Instruction section.</p><p><b>Service Instruction</b></p><p>Reference the steps below for the bank(s) leaking lubricating oil:</p><ul><li>Loosen the T bolt clamps (1) on the air crossover hose (2).</li><li>Refer to Procedure 010-019 in Section 10 to remove the air crossover.</li><li>Remove the T bolt clamps (1) from the air crossover hose (2) and remove the hose from the turbocharger compressor outlet (4).</li><li>Clean the outer surfaces of the air crossover inlet (3) and turbocharger compressor outlet (4).</li><li>Clean the inner surface of the air crossover hose (2).</li><li>Apply a bead of Loctite RTV Gasket Maker: SI 587, Part Number 135504, or equivalent, to the outer surface of the air crossover inlet (3) and the turbocharger compressor outlet (4) behind the lip of the inlet/outlet.</li><li>Install the air crossover hose (2) onto the turbocharger compressor outlet (4).</li><li>Loosely install the T bolt clamps (1) onto the hose (2).</li><li>Refer to Procedure 010-019 in Section 10 to install the air crossover (3).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10r00512.png\" alt=\"10r00512.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Example of Turbocharger Joint (LB shown).</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250118":"<h2>Obsoleting the Re-use Criteria for Crankshaft Adapter Capscrews</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>K50</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Crankshaft adapter capscrews have been found broken upon inspection and during engine rebuilds.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li><b>Always</b> replace crankshaft adapter capscrews during rebuilds and mid-life events.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6adma.png\" alt=\"ks6adma.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Crankshaft Adapter Capscrews.</p><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>K50</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K108</td><td>4332823</td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250125":"<h2>New Engine Wiring Harnesses</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM850</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>New engine wiring harnesses are released in correspondence with the new 3-pump electric fuel lift pump system.</li><li>There are now three electric fuel lift pump power connections on the engine wiring harnesses. The branch of the engine wiring harness that includes the fuel lift pump relay has also been extended in length to achieve a more accessible location when installed on-engine and increased in wire gauge to the appropriate size for the new system. Depending on the mounting location of the fuel lift pump and filter head assembly, the fuel lift pump harness assembly may be routed in one of two paths, as shown in Figures 1 and 2. The wiring diagrams have been updated for all possible engine wiring harness options.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00r01909.png\" alt=\"00r01909.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Engine Left Bank Fuel Lift Pump Relay Location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00r01910.png\" alt=\"00r01910.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Engine Rear Fuel Lift Pump Relay Location.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial Wiring Harness (Lift Pump Harness Assembly)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5544940\">5544940</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>6426736</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial Wiring Harness (Main Engine Harness Assembly)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483706\">5483706</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>6426740</td></tr><tr><td>G-Drive Wiring Harness (Main Engine Harness Assembly)</td><td>5544939</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>6426743</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Harness Part Number</td><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>6426743</td><td>37296947</td><td>5 May 2025</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>6426736</td><td>37297094</td><td>27 May 2025</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>6426740</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250131":"<h2>Steel Edge Bonded Gear Housing Gasket</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK38 CM2150 K106</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K142M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K145G</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K107</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K140M</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K146G</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A steel edge bonded rubber gasket version of the existing gear housing fiber gasket released and added as an optional addition.</li><li>The sealing of the two different gaskets differs significantly therefore the change in gasket material has also driven dimensional changes to the gear housing, gear cover and camshaft cover (gear cover) designs. A shim has also been released to ensure the air gap for the camshaft position sensor is maintained. A procedure currently exists in the service manuals to allow for shimming of the camshaft position sensor without the need for the specific OEM shim.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Improved lubrication oil sealing between the gear cover and housing and the cylinder block.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>The additional gear housing gaskets are a steel edge bonded rubber gasket and have been released alongside the existing fiber gear housing gasket which will continue to be available as a production and service item for existing engines.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Table 1 shows where the camshaft position sensor needs to be shimmed. Should an OEM shim <b>not</b> be available, a procedure does exist to machine a shim locally to meet specific requirements. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 019-363 in Section 19.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>GASKET</td><td>3644502</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5539807\">5539807</a></td></tr><tr><td>COVER, GEAR (Without Camshaft Cover)</td><td>3638995</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6389444​</td></tr><tr><td>COVER, GEAR (With Camshaft Cover)</td><td>5694541</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6389476*</td></tr><tr><td>HOUSING, GEAR</td><td>3176918</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5374581\">5374581</a></td></tr><tr><td>COVER, CAMSHAFT</td><td>5374477​</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5539411*</td></tr><tr><td>SHIM</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6398296</td></tr><tr><td>*Shim Required When Using New Part Number</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>The new steel edge bonded gaskets is <b>not</b> to be used with the fiber gasket gear covers and housings. Because of the increased thickness of the steel edge bonded gasket and narrower sealing patterns, contact interactions with the gear train and electronics exist.</li><li>When upgrading engines originally fitted with fiber gear housing gaskets to the new steel edge bonded gear housing gaskets the new gear cover and housing part numbers in Table 1 <b>must</b> be used to prevent damage to the gear train and electrical components.</li></ul><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><ul><li>New part is stainless-steel and is easily recognizable compared to the fiber gasket.</li><li>The part number permanently applied on the steel carrier of the new gaskets.</li></ul><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33239155</td><td>26 May 2025</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250144":"<h2>Metal Particle Detector</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new metal particle detector is offered which detects metal debris in the engine lubricating oil.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>The new metal particle detector can detect metal debris within the fuel pump and provide advanced warning to prevent progressive damage. After initial installation, the metal particle detector is serviceable on engine without having to remove the fuel pump.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>KIT,SERVICE</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6536874</td></tr><tr><td>HARNESS,WIRING* (optional)</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6568490</td></tr><tr><td>* Extension wiring harness only required for non-fracturing rig applications.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Installation Instructions</b></p><p>Tools Required:</p><ul><li>Blind hole puller for removing cup plug. See Figure 1.</li><li>Recommend the following or equivalent:</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01r00478.png\" alt=\"01r00478.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Blind Hole Puller.</p><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not use heel bars or punches to drive cup plug in and attempt removal; damage can occur to the camshaft lobe.</p></div></div><ol><li>Initial metal particle detector installation requires removing fuel pump from engine. For removal and installation instructions, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 005-016 in Section 5.</li></ol><ol><li>Use blind hole puller to remove center cup plug from bottom of fuel pump. See Figure 2 for cup plug location. Insert blind hole tool into cup plug until blind hole tool bottoms out. Hold expansion collet with wrench and turn actuator pin to expand collet. Tighten the actuator pin until there is firm resistance; do <b>not</b> overtighten. The collet should firmly lock into cup plug. Use slide hammer to extract cup plug.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01r00479.png\" alt=\"01r00479.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Cup Plug Location.</p><ol><li>Inspect cylinder block to confirm cylinder block lubricating oil pan rail is chamfered. See Figure 3. If chamfered edge is missing, follow Step 4 for field modification. Chamfer is required for metal particle detector removal and install clearance without removing fuel pump.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01r00480.png\" alt=\"01r00480.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Chamfer.</p><ol><li>If chamfered edge is missing, locate third capscrew on cylinder block lubricating oil pan rail back from fuel pump mount. Use die grinder with metal cutting bit to create chamfer. Chamfer cylinder block just above third bolt – 25 mm [ 1 in ] wide and 6 mm [ 1/4 in ] deep. Cover fuel pump drive to prevent debris ingress. Clean any metal debris thoroughly to prevent metal particle detector contamination, as the sensor is magnetic.</li></ol><ol><li>Use thread restorer or bottom tap to clean caprscrew hole threads before fuel pump install. Metal particle detector mounting hole <b>must</b> be clean of debris to allow proper ground bond for sensor operation.</li></ol><ol><li>Install fuel pump. Reference Procedure 005-016 in Section 5. With cup plug removed, the step for adding lubricating oil to pre-lube the pump during installation will <b>not</b> be possible. Follow recommended fuel pump pre-lube process in Step 8.</li></ol><ol><li>Insert metal particle detector into number 3 cup plug cavity, see Figure 4. Install supplied spacer between metal particle detector and cylinder block. Install capscrew with washer and torque.</li></ol><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>54 n•m [40 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01r00481.png\" alt=\"01r00481.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Metal Particle Detector Installed</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02483.png\" alt=\"17r02483.png\"></figure><p>Figure 5, Side View Showing Removal Clearance from Chamfer</p><ol><li>Before starting engine, fuel pump <b>must</b> be pre-lubed with engine oil. If a factory pre-lube system is installed, cycle before starting the engine. If there is no pre-lube system, disconnect injector wiring harnesses at the 8-pin connectors to disable fueling and crank engine to build oil pressure. Once engine oil pressure is observed, reconnect the 8-pin connectors.</li></ol><ol><li>Many fracturing units already have a wiring interface. Before installing the metal particle detector in other applications, contact your HHP CFSE for wiring interface options.</li></ol><p>An extension wiring harness is available for non-Frac rig applications, see Table 1. The extension harness connects to the current engine wiring harness at the connector for the ambient air pressure sensor. There is a branch on the extension harness to reconnect the ambient air pressure sensor, as well as a branch to connect to the metal particle detector.</p><ol><li>The OEM/Customer is responsible for integration of the sensor signal into their controls for notification of metal particle detector activation when debris is present. The metal particle detector lead provides a ground reference when metallic debris is present on sensor.</li></ol><p>Once connected, the electronic chip detector will set off Fault Code 221 or 222. There is <b>not</b> a dedicated fault code or lamp within the engine software for the electronic chip detector.</p><p>Various forms of integration have been observed for oil and gas applications. Examples are shutting down engine or driving transmission to instant neutral to notify operator of sensor activation. If the OEM/Customer wants the engine or vehicle to automatically respond, the response will need to be programmed into the vehicle controls.</p><ol><li>The metal particle detector can be field removed and inspected when activated. The sensor face should <b>not</b> contain metallic debris buildup. Any metal debris that bridges the gap between the sensing washer and body will activate a ground reference signal.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02484.png\" alt=\"17r02484.png\"></figure><p>Figure 6, Removed Metal Particle Detector</p><p>Metal Particle Detector Operation and Maintenance:</p><ul><li>A single piece of metal debris can activate the metal particle detector. This can be classified as a false activation, but repeated activation indicates a metal debris generating condition has occurred within the engine. <b>Not</b> all metal particle detector activations are indication of just fuel pump malfunction, any debris circulation in lube system can be detected by metal particle detector. Repeated activation of metal particle detector will require diagnostics to determine source of debris.</li><li>Metal particle detector surface can be wiped clean or cleaned with a non-metallic brush. Once the metal particle detector is clean, test using resistance function on multimeter. Metal particle detector can be installed or removed for testing. Test should reference metal particle detector lead to metal particle detector body. A clean metal particle detector will read greater than 10 MΩ.</li></ul><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250157":"<h2>Turbocharger Speed Sensor and Crankshaft Position Sensor Update</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>HSK78G CM2358 K129G</li><li>HSK78G CM2358 K171C</li><li>K38 CM2350 K165G</li><li>QSK23 CM2250 K109</li><li>QSK23 CM2350 K127</li><li>QSK23 CM2450 K168G</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K106</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 XPI</li><li>QSK38 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K146G</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2250 K112</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K149M</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 G CM2358 K110</li><li>QSK78 CM2250 K104</li><li>QSK78 CM2250 K126</li><li>QSK78 CM2250 K137</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K154C</li><li>QSK78 CM2450 K161G</li><li>QSK78 CM500 MCRS</li><li>QSK95 CM2350 K111</li><li>QSK95 CM2350 K113</li><li>QSK95 CM2350 K128M</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>The anodizing layer on the inner side of the aluminum body of the turbocharger speed sensor and crankshaft position has been removed.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>The anodized aluminum material compromised the epoxy, creating a leak path for water ingress. This caused shorting of the internal electronics, resulting in sensor signal loss, electronic control module (ECM) fault codes, and engine shutdown.</li></ul><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><ul><li>The change is documented on the sensor using the printed date code. Date codes as follows:</li><li>Crankshaft position sensor part number, <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5491808\">5491808</a> - Date Code: 25062</li><li>Turbocharger speed sensor part number, 5491809 - Date Code: 25230</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10r00513.png\" alt=\"10r00513.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Crankshaft Position Sensor Date Code Location.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33238971</td><td>2 May 2025</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250191":"<h2>Fuel Injection Pump Bracket Countermeasure</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Fuel injection pump mounting bracket and P-clip capscrew loosens, sometimes leading to fuel injection mounting bracket fracture (see Figure 1).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02466.png\" alt=\"17r02466.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Example of loosened capscrew and broken fuel injection mounting bracket</p><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>The isolated spot-facing of the fuel injection mounting bracket creates a step that interferes with the P clip seating, leading to bending of the P-clip, progressive preload loss of the joint, capscrew loosening and possible fuel injection mounting bracket fracture.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>This issue pertains to any engine built with fuel injection mounting bracket Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093263\">3093263</a> with a P-clip mounted in the location shown in Figure 2. This will be found on the left bank side of the engine.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02467.png\" alt=\"17r02467.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Example of P-clip mounting location</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>First, verify there is a washer present between the P-clip and capscrew head. If there is no washer present, washer Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3094024\">3094024</a> (1), will need to be installed between the capscrew head and P-clip.</p><p>Additionally, washer Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093799\">3093799</a> (2), should be installed between the P-clip and the fuel injection mounting bracket surface, as shown in Figure 3.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02468.png\" alt=\"17r02468.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Washer Countermeasure Installation</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 1.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>ESN First</b></td><td><b>Build Date</b> 1</td><td><b>Plant</b></td></tr><tr><td>37297911</td><td>3 September 2025</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250197":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Suction Tube for Center-Deep, Industrial Application</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>For all QST30 CM552 Industrial applications, the <b>only</b> center-deep oil pan option available is OP5151, which includes lubricating oil suction tube bracket, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4310907\">4310907</a>. This is the most robust lubricating oil suction tube for the center-deep lubricating oil pan configuration for the QST30.</p><p>For any lubricating oil suction tube failures on Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093694\">3093694</a> in the industrial application, replace with Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4310907\">4310907</a>.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>This change is a result of lubricating oil suction tube failures with part number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093694\">3093694</a> within option OP5133.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Suction Tube</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093694\">3093694</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4310907\">4310907</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250205":"<h2>Belt Guard Noise</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Loud rattling noise coming from front of engine.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Loose/cracked belt guard.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Verify the belt guard has loose capscrews or cracks in belt guard.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>If a loose belt guard is observed, the belt guard can be removed and reinstalled with alternate isolators. If the belt guard is also cracked, the belt guard should be replaced when the alternate isolators are installed. See QST30 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a>. Refer to Procedure 008-001 in Section 8 to remove belt guard. Follow modifications described in this bulletin to install the belt guard.</p><p><b>Service Instruction</b></p><p>See QST30 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a>. Refer to Procedure 008-001 in Section 8 to remove belt guard.</p><p>Install:</p><ol><li>Install the capscrew assemblies onto the belt guard. The components <b>must</b> be in the order shown.</li></ol><p><b>Three mounting locations will each use:</b> 1. Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093807\">3093807</a> – capscrew – quantity 1 2. Part Number 3643019 – retaining ring – quantity 1 (<b>Not</b> visible in picture, located between bracket and first washer) 3. Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035083\">3035083</a> – vibration isolator – quantity 1 4. Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035084\">3035084</a> – vibration isolator – quantity 1 5. Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3033800\">3033800</a> – washer – quantity 2</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13f00011.png\" alt=\"13f00011.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Capscrew Assembly with Three Mounting Locations</p><p><b>One mounting location (bottom) will use:</b> 1. Part Number 3641716 – capscrew – quantity 1 2. Part Number 3643019 – retaining ring – quantity 1 (<b>Not</b> visible in picture, located between bracket and first washer) 3. Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035083\">3035083</a> – vibration isolator – quantity 1 4. Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035084\">3035084</a> – vibration isolator– quantity 1 5. Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3033800\">3033800</a> – washer – quantity 2 6. Part Number 3072999 – spacer – quantity 1</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13f00012.png\" alt=\"13f00012.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Capscrew Assembly with One Mounting Location</p><ol><li>Apply Loctite to capscrews.</li></ol><ol><li>Install the belt guard.</li></ol><ol><li>Tighten the capscrew assemblies until the distance between the washers is 9.5 mm [ 0.37 in ].</li></ol><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Washer</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3033800\">3033800</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>N/A</td></tr><tr><td>Isolator</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035083\">3035083</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>N/A</td></tr><tr><td>Isolator</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035084\">3035084</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>N/A</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250208":"<h2>New Turbocharger</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new turbocharger has been released to production and the aftermarket for the QST30 CM552 product. The change includes a new turbocharger bearing housing design with new internal lubricating oil drain geometry to address lubricating oil leaks from the turbocharger compressor outlet.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>There is a possibility when installing the new turbocharger that the turbocharger oil drain line flange could interfere with one of the turbocharger turbine housing capscrews. If the turbocharger oil drain line flange can <b>not</b> be seated flat against the mounting surface of the turbocharger oil drain outlet, the turbocharger oil drain line flange will need to be modified using the following instructions.</p><p>See the QST30 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a>. Refer to Procedure 010-045 in Section 10 to remove and clean the turbocharger oil drain line. Use Clean Care Kit protective caps to keep the engine block oil drain port clean.</p><p>With the turbocharger oil drain line removed and cleaned of all residual oil, use tape to cover the inlet and outlet of the turbocharger oil drain line to prevent introduction of debris into oil drain path.</p><p>Using a hand file, remove approximately 1.5 mm [ 0.06 in ] from the interfering side of the turbocharger oil drain line flange. See Figure 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10f00110.png\" alt=\"10f00110.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Visualization of removed material from turbocharger oil drain line flange.</p><p>After material has been removed, remove tape from the inlet and outlet of the turbocharger oil drain line.</p><p>See the QST30 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a>. Refer to procedure 010-045 in Section 10 to install the turbocharger oil drain line.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Aftermarket Turbocharger Kit</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5321615\">5321615</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6597459</td></tr><tr><td>Turbocharger</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4042918\">4042918</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6596568</td></tr><tr><td>Turbocharger</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4042919\">4042919</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6596569</td></tr><tr><td>Aftermarket Turbocharger</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4033508\">4033508</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6597458</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Engine Model</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37298583</td><td>QST30 CM552</td><td>24 November 2025</td><td>SEP</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Release strategy is under review.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250219":"<h2>Low Temperature Aftercooler (LTA) Water Pump O-ring Oil Leak Countermeasure</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><p>QST30 CM552</p><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>External oil leak from the LTA water pump-to-accessory drive joint.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>The o-ring seal of this joint experiences temperature exposure beyond its material capabilities.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Verify you have an accessory drive-driven LTA water pump system, which includes options DF5749 or DF5752 paired with options TB5820 or TB5828</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>As a countermeasure for this issue, replace failed seal part number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025376\">4025376</a> with seal part number 3033248, which has higher temperature exposure capabilities. See image 1 for referenced o-ring seal.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08f00171.png\" alt=\"08f00171.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Example of LTA water pump mounting o-ring seal</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL,O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025376\">4025376</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>3033248</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250220":"<h2>Inactive Fault Code 173</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Multiple counts of Fault Code 173 will show as inactive after attempting to start the engine.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Some calibrations will activate and deactivate this fault code while attempting to start the engine.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>This issue applies to engines with engine control module (ECM) Codes K50042.XX, K50204.XX or K50220.XX. Connect to the ECM with recommended Cummins® Electronic Service Tool or equivalent and verify the ECM Code.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If Fault Code 173 continually reappears as inactive, this is a nuisance fault code and can be ignored. If Fault Code173 is showing as active, follow the standard troubleshooting steps.</li></ul><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb260032":"<h2>New Lubricating Oil Pan Gasket</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM850</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T103C</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A lubricating oil pan gasket is being released as a directional improvement to the sealing of the joint. The new lubricating oil pan gasket has maintained the same 8-piece design but is 0.6mm thinner and a new material.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Make sure the lubricating oil pan gasket is installed with the “PAN SIDE” stamped text facing the lubricating oil pan.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Pan Gasket</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092855\">3092855</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6627646</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The new lubricating oil pan gasket is approximately 1.0mm in thickness and black in color. It has the text “PAN SIDE” stamped on one side of each individual piece.</p><p>The current lubricating oil pan gasket is approximately 1.6mm in thickness and blue in color. It has text including “TS-9016” printed multiple times on one side of each individual piece.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02604.png\" alt=\"17r02604.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, New Lubricating Oil Pan Gasket</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02605.png\" alt=\"17r02605.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Current Lubricating Oil Pan Gasket</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Obsolete part numbers in stock are <b>not</b> to be used. Obsolete part numbers in stock are to be returned for reimbursement.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37298055</td><td>30 September 2025</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3. Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a></td><td>Lubricating Oil Pan</td><td>Refer to Procedure 007-025</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM2350 T101</td><td>5467179</td><td>Lubricating Oil Pan</td><td>Refer to Procedure 007-025</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM2350 T103C</td><td>6309062</td><td>Lubricating Oil Pan</td><td>Refer to Procedure 007-025</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb260059":"<h2>Low-Temperature Aftercooler (LTA) Accessory Drive Housing Improvement</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><h4>Symptom:</h4><ul><li>Lubricating oil leak from joint between offset accessory drive and low temperature aftercooler (LTA) water pump drive.</li></ul><h4>Root Cause:</h4><ul><li>Press in place seal installed incorrectly.</li><li>Surface finish of offset accessory drive mating surface machined incorrectly.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Verify you have an accessory drive driven LTA water pump system, which includes DF5749 or DF5752 with options TB5820 or TB5828. Verify external lubricating oil leak originates from press in place seal joint and <b>not</b> from adjacent joints.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Inspect offset accessory drive and LTA water pump drive for damaged threads and excessively rough sealing surfaces. Replace any damaged components. Replace press in place seal. Verify that press in place seal is fully seated in seal groove.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/12n00093.png\" alt=\"12n00093.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Example of Rough Sealing Surface on Accessory Drive</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/12n00094.png\" alt=\"12n00094.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Press in Place Seal Fully Seated in Seal Groove</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>DRIVE, ACCESSORY</td><td>5415281</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>N/A</td></tr><tr><td>DRIVE, ACCESSORY</td><td>5409383</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>N/A</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, RECTANGULAR STRIP</td><td>4390009</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>N/A</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37299741</td><td>27 April 2026</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb260073":"<h2>CES14648 – Coolant Specification Introduction</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>GTA855 CM558 G101</li><li>K19 C</li><li>K19 M</li><li>K19 CM2670 K213C</li><li>K38 C</li><li>K38 M</li><li>K50 C</li><li>K50 M</li><li>QSK19 CM2150 K139M</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K105</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K114</li><li>QSK23 CM2250 K109</li><li>QSK23 CM2350 K127</li><li>QSK23 CM2670 K174C</li><li>QSK23 CM2450 K211</li><li>QSK38 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K106</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K142M</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K169M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM2670 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2670 K193M</li><li>QSK38 CM2670 K202C</li><li>QSK38 CM2670 K205M</li><li>QSK38 CM2670 K206M</li><li>QSK45 CM500</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K107</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K140M</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K163C</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K216C</li><li>QSK50 CM2670 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2670 K175C</li><li>QSK50 CM2670 K176C</li><li>QSK50 CM2670 K207</li><li>QSK60 CM2670 K208M</li><li>QSK60 CM500</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 K132M</li><li>QSK60 CM2250 K112</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K133</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K149M</li><li>QSK60 CM2670 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2670 K177C</li><li>QSK60 CM2670 K178C</li><li>QSK60 CM2670 K179C</li><li>QSK60 CM2670 K181C</li><li>QSK60 CM2670 K183M</li><li>QSK78 CM500</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K126</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K137</li><li>QSK78 CM2670 K184C</li><li>QSK78 CM2670 K185C</li><li>QSK78 CM2670 K186C</li><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T102</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T103C</li><li>QST30 CM2450 T105C</li><li>V903</li><li>V903 CM2250 V106C</li><li>VTA903 CM2250 V105</li><li>VTA903 CM2250 V107C</li><li>VTA903 CM2250</li></ul><p><b>Issue</b></p><p>Organic Additive Technology (OAT) Coolants have a broad range of base and secondary formulas that meet Cummins Engineering Standard (CES) CES14603. Some formulas that meet CES14603 have been shown to <b>not</b> be capable of protection against liner cavitation in Cummins Inc High Horsepower (HHP) engines with Top Stop Wet Cylinder Liners. The incompatibility can be caused by two main factors:</p><ul><li>Omission of critical chemical additives that are required for cavitation protection</li><li>Inclusion of chemicals that are <b>not</b> fully compatible with Cylinder Liner materials.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>CES14648 has been developed around HHP Top Stop Wet Liners to improve cylinder liner protection against cavitation. This CES has been applied to Industrial and Marine applications <b>only</b>. A full list of affected engine platforms is available in the following manuals and procedures.</p><ul><li><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/5411406\">5411406</a> –Fluids for Cummins® Products Service Manual &gt; Section 5 – Coolant:</li><li>All Cummins Inc. Engine Platform Owners Manuals &gt; Section V – Maintenance Specifications:</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Cummins has conducted substantial rig and field testing of Nitrite-Free propylene Glycol based coolants. The results of this testing have determined:</p><ul><li>Propylene glycol based coolants that contain nitrite or nitrite and molybdate additives within a certain concentration range have been shown to be effective in preventing liner cavitation in HHP Engines with Top Stop Wet Cylinder Liners.</li><li>Propylene glycol based coolants that contain nitrite or nitrite and molybdate additives below specific concentration limits, or that omit these additives entirely, have been found to lead to premature liner cavitation in HHP Engines with Top Stop Wet Liners.</li></ul><p>As a result of this testing, Cummins has introduced a new Cummins Engineering Standard (CES) CES14648 for coolants used in HHP Engines with Top Stop Wet Liners. CES 14648 requires propylene glycol based coolants to contain nitrite or nitrite and molybdate at the concentrations specified by ASTM D6210, Table A1.1.</p><p>CES 14648 permits the use of ethylene glycol based coolants that do <b>not</b> contain any nitrite or molybdate.</p><p>Visit the CES 14648 registered products page on QSOL to select an appropriate coolant.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><a href=\"https://quickserve.cummins.com/qs3/qsol/service/serviceproducts/coolant_registration.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://quickserve.cummins.com/qs3/qsol/service/serviceproducts/coolant_registration.html</a></p></div></div><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>CEAF 202605200001 submitted and issued.</p><p>Fluids for Cummins Engines Manual, Section 5, (<a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-379-004\">00-379-004</a>) Engine Coolant Requirements and (<a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-379-006\">00-379-006</a>) Maintenance of Coolant and Cooling System. All Cummins Inc Engine Platform Owners Manuals, Section V – Maintenance Specifications, (<a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-018-004\">00-018-004</a>) Coolant Recommendations and Specifications are updated.</p><ul><li>These manuals serve as &quot;high level&quot; documents. The lower-level service manual documents (Owner, Operation and Maintenance, Service) in all individual engine configurations contain conflicts with the updated high-level documents. Where these conflicts occur, the High-Level documentation takes precedence.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Follow Service Manual guidelines. Propylene glycol (PG) based coolants <b>must</b> contain nitrite or nitrite and molybdate within the specified concentration limits in ASTM D6210 Table A1.1. PG based OAT coolants <b>not</b> meeting these requirements should be removed at the earliest opportunity and replaced with a compliant coolant.</p><p>Fluids for Cummins Engines Manual, Section 5, (<a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-379-004\">00-379-004</a>) Engine Coolant Requirements and (<a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-379-006\">00-379-006</a>) Maintenance of Coolant and Cooling System. All Cummins Inc Engine Platform Owners Manuals, Section V – Maintenance Specifications, (<a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-018-004\">00-018-004</a>) Coolant Recommendations and Specifications are updated as these serve as ‘high level&#x27; documents. The lower-level service manual documents (Owner, Operation and Maintenance, Service) in all individual engine configurations contain conflicts with the updated high-level documents. Where these conflicts occur, the High-Level documentation takes precedence.</p><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Fluids for Cummins Engines Manual</td><td>See Affected Product List</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/5411406\">5411406</a></td><td>Engine Coolant Requirements</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-379-004\">00-379-004</a></td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Fluids for Cummins Engines Manual</td><td>See Affected Product List</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/5411406\">5411406</a></td><td>Maintenance of Coolant and Cooling System</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-379-006\">00-379-006</a></td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Owners Manuals</td><td>All Cummins Inc Owners Manuals within Technical Service Bulletin, Cummins® Service Model Identification, <a class=\"lnk tsb\" href=\"#/doc/tsb130080\">TSB130080</a></td><td></td><td>Maintenance Specifications</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/00-018-004\">00-018-004</a></td><td>V</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb260089":"<h2>Vibration Damper Guard</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Loud rattling noise coming from front of engine.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Loose/cracked vibration damper guard.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Verify the vibration damper guard has loose capscrews or cracks in vibration damper guard.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If a loose or cracked vibration damper guard is observed, there is a new vibration damper guard available. The new vibration damper guard is made from thicker sheet steel and is modified so that isolators can be installed. Longer capscrews and washers are used with the isolators.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Remove:</p><ol><li>Remove the loose/cracked vibration damper guard.</li></ol><p>Install:</p><ol><li>Install the capscrew assemblies onto the vibration damper guard. The washer used varies by mounting location. The isolator <b>must</b> be located between the vibration damper guard and the engine.</li></ol><p>The top mounting locations will each use:</p><ul><li>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3920447\">3920447</a> – capscrew – quantity 1</li><li>Part Number 4975786 – retaining ring – quantity 1(<b>Not</b> visible in picture, locate between bracket and first washer)</li><li>Part Number 3041247 – plain washer – quantity 1</li><li>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035083\">3035083</a> – isolator – quantity 1</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02705.png\" alt=\"17r02705.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Capscrew Assembly for Top Mounting Locations</p><p>The bottom right mounting location will use:</p><ul><li>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3920447\">3920447</a> – capscrew – quantity 1</li><li>Part Number 4975786 – retaining ring – quantity 1(<b>Not</b> visible in picture, locate between bracket and first washer)</li><li>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035806\">3035806</a> – plain washer – quantity 1</li><li>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035083\">3035083</a> – isolator – quantity 1</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02706.png\" alt=\"17r02706.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Capscrew Assembly for Bottom Right Mounting Location</p><p>The bottom left mounting location will use:</p><ul><li>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3920447\">3920447</a> – capscrew – quantity 1</li><li>Part Number 4975786 – retaining ring – quantity 1 (<b>Not</b> visible in picture, locate between bracket and isolator)</li><li>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035083\">3035083</a> – isolator – quantity 1</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02707.png\" alt=\"17r02707.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Capscrew Assembly for Bottom Left Mounting Location</p><ol><li>Apply Loctite® to capscrew threads.</li></ol><ol><li>Position the vibration damper guard onto the engine and hand start the capscrews in the top mounting locations.</li></ol><ol><li>Hand start the capscrew in the bottom right location. The washer and isolator will be off center for clearance to the cover plate.</li></ol><ol><li>Hand start the capscrew in the bottom left location through the vibration damper guard and breather tube bracket.</li></ol><ol><li>Tighten capscrew assemblies until the isolator thickness is 2 mm [ 0.079 in ] nominal.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02708.png\" alt=\"17r02708.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Top Left Mounting Location after Install</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02709.png\" alt=\"17r02709.png\"></figure><p>Figure 5, Top Right Mounting Location after Install</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02710.png\" alt=\"17r02710.png\"></figure><p>Figure 6, Bottom Right Mounting Location after Install</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02711.png\" alt=\"17r02711.png\"></figure><p>Figure 7, Bottom Left Mounting Location after Install</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Vibration Damper Guard</td><td>4975804</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6636278</td></tr><tr><td>Isolator</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035083\">3035083</a></td></tr><tr><td>Plain Washer</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3035806\">3035806</a></td></tr><tr><td>Plain Washer</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>3041247</td></tr><tr><td>Hex Flange Head Capscrew</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093939\">3093939</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3920447\">3920447</a></td></tr><tr><td>Retaining Ring</td><td>4975786</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>4975786</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>"};
